<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="section-mass-center-of-mass" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Applications: Mass and Center of Mass</title>

<introduction>
<p>
Multiple integrals let us compute physical quantities of solid objects
and flat plates whose <term>density</term> varies from point to point.
In this section we use double and triple integrals to compute the total
mass of an object and to locate its <term>center of mass</term><mdash/>the
point at which the object would balance perfectly. Before we study
continuous objects, we review what the center of mass of a system of
point masses is and why it deserves its name: first for two masses, then
for any number of them. A flat plate and a solid are then handled in the
same way, by cutting them into many small pieces and treating each piece
as a point mass.
</p>
<p>
Starred material is optional. In this section that is
<xref ref="sec-mass-3d-object"/>, on point masses in space and solid
objects.
</p>
</introduction>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-com-two-masses">
<title>The Center of Mass of Two Point Masses</title>

<p>
Consider two masses <m>m_1</m> and <m>m_2</m> located at the points
<m>\mathbf{r}_1 = (x_1, y_1)</m> and <m>\mathbf{r}_2 = (x_2, y_2)</m>, as
in <xref ref="fig-two-masses"/>, and let <m>\mathbf{F}_1</m> and
<m>\mathbf{F}_2</m> be the net forces acting on them. By Newton's second
law, each force is the mass times the acceleration,
<m>\mathbf{F}_1 = m_1\, \dfrac{d^2\mathbf{r}_1}{dt^2}</m> and
<m>\mathbf{F}_2 = m_2\, \dfrac{d^2\mathbf{r}_2}{dt^2}</m>, so the total
force on the system of the two masses is
</p>

<me>
\mathbf{F} = \mathbf{F}_1 + \mathbf{F}_2
= m_1 \frac{d^2\mathbf{r}_1}{dt^2} + m_2 \frac{d^2\mathbf{r}_2}{dt^2}
= \frac{d^2}{dt^2}\left( \sum_{i=1}^{2} m_i\, \mathbf{r}_i \right).
</me>

<p>
It would be nice to rewrite this in the familiar form
<m>\mathbf{F} = M\mathbf{a}</m> of Newton's second law. To this end, we
multiply and divide the right-hand side by the total mass
<m>M = m_1 + m_2</m> of the system:
</p>

<me>
\mathbf{F} = M\, \frac{d^2}{dt^2}\left( \sum_{i=1}^{2} \frac{m_i}{M}\, \mathbf{r}_i \right)
= M\, \frac{d^2\bar{\mathbf{r}}}{dt^2},
</me>

<p>
where we have defined the <term>center of mass</term> of the system as
the point
</p>

<men xml:id="eq-com-two-masses">
\bar{\mathbf{r}} = \frac{1}{M} \sum_{i=1}^{2} m_i\, \mathbf{r}_i
= \left( \frac{m_1 x_1 + m_2 x_2}{m_1 + m_2},\
         \frac{m_1 y_1 + m_2 y_2}{m_1 + m_2} \right).
</men>

<p>
Thus the total force equals the total mass times the acceleration of a
virtual mass <m>M</m> located at <m>\bar{\mathbf{r}}</m>: as far as the
net force is concerned, the pair of masses moves like a single particle
of mass <m>M</m> sitting at the center of mass. The formula is a weighted
average of the two positions, with the masses as the weights. If
<m>m_1 = m_2</m>, the center of mass is the midpoint of the segment
joining the two masses; otherwise it lies on that segment, closer to the
heavier mass.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-two-masses">
<caption>
Two point masses <m>m_1</m> and <m>m_2</m> at the points
<m>\mathbf{r}_1</m> and <m>\mathbf{r}_2</m>, with the net forces
<m>\mathbf{F}_1</m> and <m>\mathbf{F}_2</m> acting on them. Their center
of mass <m>\bar{\mathbf{r}}</m> lies on the segment joining them, closer
to the heavier mass (here <m>m_2 = 2m_1</m>).
</caption>
<image width="60%">
<shortdescription>
Two masses in the plane with their position vectors, downward force
arrows, and the center of mass on the segment between them.
</shortdescription>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-two-masses">
<diagram dimensions="(360,280)" margins="10">
<coordinates bbox="(-0.6,-1.2,4.6,3.0)">
