</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-com-n-masses">
<title>The Center of Mass of <m>n</m> Point Masses and the First Moments</title>

<p>
Nothing in the computation above depended on there being only two
masses. For a system of <m>n</m> point masses <m>m_1, \dots, m_n</m> at
the points <m>\mathbf{r}_1, \dots, \mathbf{r}_n</m>, adding Newton's
second law for each mass and multiplying and dividing by the total mass
<m>M = m_1 + \cdots + m_n</m> gives, exactly as before,
<m>\mathbf{F} = M\, \dfrac{d^2\bar{\mathbf{r}}}{dt^2}</m> with
</p>

<men xml:id="eq-com-points-vector">
\bar{\mathbf{r}} = \frac{1}{M} \sum_{i=1}^{n} m_i\, \mathbf{r}_i,
\qquad M = \sum_{i=1}^{n} m_i .
</men>

<p>
The sums in the coordinates of <m>\bar{\mathbf{r}}</m> have names of
their own. For masses in the plane, with <m>\mathbf{r}_i = (x_i, y_i)</m>,
the <term>first moments</term> of the system are
</p>

<men xml:id="eq-first-moments-points">
M_y = \sum_{i=1}^{n} m_i\, x_i, \qquad
M_x = \sum_{i=1}^{n} m_i\, y_i .
</men>

<p>
<m>M_y</m> is the <term>moment about the <m>y</m>-axis</term>: each mass
is multiplied by its signed distance <m>x_i</m> from the <m>y</m>-axis,
so <m>M_y</m> measures the tendency of the system to tip about that axis
(masses to the right of the axis, with <m>x_i \gt 0</m>, count
positively, and masses to its left count negatively). Likewise
<m>M_x</m> is the <term>moment about the <m>x</m>-axis</term>, computed
with the signed distances <m>y_i</m> from the <m>x</m>-axis. In this
notation, <xref ref="eq-com-points-vector"/> says that the center of
mass <m>\bar{\mathbf{r}} = (\bar{x}, \bar{y})</m> has the coordinates
</p>

<men xml:id="eq-com-points">
\bar{x} = \frac{M_y}{M}, \qquad
\bar{y} = \frac{M_x}{M}.
</men>

<p>
Every formula for a flat plate in the next subsection is one of these,
with the sum over point masses replaced by an integral over the plate.
Masses in space, and solid objects, are treated in
<xref ref="sec-mass-3d-object"/>.
</p>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-mass-2d-object">
<title>Finding the Center of Mass of a 2D Object</title>

<p>
Now let the mass be spread continuously over a flat plate that occupies a
region <m>R</m> in the plane, with <term>density</term>
<m>\delta(x,y)</m> (mass per unit area) at the point <m>(x,y)</m>. To
bring the formulas of the previous subsection to bear, we cut the plate
into small pieces and treat each piece as a point mass. Partition the
plane into small rectangles by the lines <m>x = x_0, x_1, \dots, x_n</m>
and <m>y = y_0, y_1, \dots, y_m</m>, as in
<xref ref="fig-plate-element"/>. The rectangle in column <m>i</m> and
row <m>j</m> has width <m>\Delta x_i</m>, height <m>\Delta y_j</m>, and
area <m>\Delta A_{ij} = \Delta x_i\, \Delta y_j</m>; if it meets
<m>R</m>, we pick a point <m>(x_i, y_j)</m> in it. The density is nearly
constant on such a small piece, so the mass of the piece is approximately
</p>

<me>
\Delta m_{ij} = \delta(x_i, y_j)\, \Delta A_{ij}
= \delta(x_i, y_j)\, \Delta x_i\, \Delta y_j .
</me>

<figure xml:id="fig-plate-element">
<caption>
A flat plate <m>R</m> cut into small rectangles. The rectangle in column
<m>i</m> and row <m>j</m> has the area <m>\Delta A_{ij} = \Delta x_i\,
\Delta y_j</m>, contains the point <m>(x_i, y_j)</m>, and carries the
mass <m>\Delta m_{ij} = \delta(x_i, y_j)\, \Delta A_{ij}</m>.
</caption>
<image width="60%">
<shortdescription>
A blob-shaped region over a grid of small rectangles, with one rectangle
inside it highlighted.
</shortdescription>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-plate-element">
<diagram dimensions="(360,300)" margins="10">
<definition>blob(t) = (5.2 + 3.1*cos(t) + 0.45*cos(2*t), 4.4 + 2.2*sin(t) - 0.35*sin(2*t) + 0.25*cos(3*t))</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-0.8,-0.8,10.4,8.6)">
<parametric-curve at="pe-region" function="blob" domain="(0,2*pi)"
                  closed="yes" fill="#cfe0f5" stroke="#0072B2"
                  thickness="2.5"/>
<grid at="pe-grid" spacings="((0,1,10),(0,1,8))" stroke="#9aa6b2" thickness="0.6"/>
<axes at="pe-axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" arrows="1"/>
<rectangle at="pe-cell" lower-left="(6,4)" dimensions="(1,1)"
           fill="#cc79a7" stroke="#7a3e62" thickness="1"/>
<point at="pe-pt" p="(6.5,4.5)" fill="black" size="3"/>
<label at="pe-R" anchor="(3.0,5.8)" alignment="c" clear-background="yes"
       background-margin="3">
<m>R</m>
</label>
<label at="pe-dx" anchor="(6.5,3.9)" alignment="s" clear-background="yes"
       background-margin="2">
<m>\Delta x_i</m>
</label>
<label at="pe-dy" anchor="(5.9,4.5)" alignment="w" clear-background="yes"
       background-margin="2">
<m>\Delta y_j</m>
</label>
<line at="pe-lead1" endpoints="((6.5,4.5),(8.2,2.3))" stroke="#555555"
      thickness="0.8"/>
<label at="pe-pt-lab" anchor="(8.25,2.25)" alignment="se"
       clear-background="yes" background-margin="3">
<m>(x_i, y_j)</m>
</label>
<line at="pe-lead2" endpoints="((6.5,5),(6.2,7.5))" stroke="#555555"
      thickness="0.8"/>
<label at="pe-mass-lab" anchor="(6.2,7.6)" alignment="n"
       clear-background="yes" background-margin="3">
<m>\Delta m_{ij} = \delta(x_i, y_j)\,\Delta A_{ij}</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A blob-shaped region R, shaded light blue, lying over a grid of small squares in the first quadrant. One square inside the region is highlighted in purple; a dot at its center is the point x sub i comma y sub j, and a leader line labels the mass of the square, delta m sub i j equals delta of x sub i comma y sub j times delta A sub i j. Its width and height are labeled delta x sub i and delta y sub j.">
  <annotation ref="pe-region" text="The plate, a blob-shaped region R shaded light blue."/>
  <annotation ref="pe-grid" text="The grid of lines x equals x sub i and y equals y sub j that cuts the plane, and the plate, into small rectangles."/>
  <annotation ref="pe-cell" text="One small rectangle inside R, highlighted in purple, with width delta x sub i and height delta y sub j."/>
  <annotation ref="pe-pt" text="The point x sub i comma y sub j chosen inside the highlighted rectangle."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
The plate is now approximated by a system of point masses
<m>\Delta m_{ij}</m> located at the points <m>(x_i, y_j)</m>, one for
each rectangle that meets <m>R</m>. By <xref ref="eq-com-points-vector"/>
and <xref ref="eq-first-moments-points"/>, the total mass and the first
moments of this system are the double sums
</p>

<md>
<mrow>M \amp\approx \sum_{j=1}^{m}\sum_{i=1}^{n}
  \delta(x_i, y_j)\, \Delta x_i\, \Delta y_j,</mrow>
<mrow>M_y \amp\approx \sum_{j=1}^{m}\sum_{i=1}^{n}
  x_i\, \delta(x_i, y_j)\, \Delta x_i\, \Delta y_j, \qquad
M_x \approx \sum_{j=1}^{m}\sum_{i=1}^{n}
  y_j\, \delta(x_i, y_j)\, \Delta x_i\, \Delta y_j,</mrow>
</md>

<p>
where the sums run over the rectangles that meet <m>R</m>. These are
Riemann sums. Letting the rectangles shrink, that is, letting
<m>m, n \to \infty</m>, turns each double sum into a double integral over
<m>R</m>. For the mass,
</p>

<men xml:id="eq-mass-2d">
M = \lim_{m,n \to \infty} \sum_{j=1}^{m}\sum_{i=1}^{n}
  \delta(x_i, y_j)\, \Delta x_i\, \Delta y_j
  = \iint_R \delta(x,y)\, dA,
</men>

<p>
and the first moments become double integrals in the same way. So the
mass and the first moments of the plate are
</p>

<men xml:id="eq-first-moments-2d">
M = \iint_R \delta\, dA, \qquad
M_y = \iint_R x\, \delta\, dA, \qquad
M_x = \iint_R y\, \delta\, dA,
</men>

<p>
where, as before, <m>M_y</m> is the moment about the <m>y</m>-axis and
<m>M_x</m> the moment about the <m>x</m>-axis, and the center of mass
is
</p>

<men xml:id="eq-com-2d">
\bar{x} = \frac{M_y}{M}, \qquad
\bar{y} = \frac{M_x}{M}.
</men>

<definition xml:id="def-centroid">
<statement>
<p>
When the density of a plate or of a solid object is constant, the center
of mass is called the <term>centroid</term> of the object.
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>
A constant density <m>\delta</m> cancels in the quotients of
<xref ref="eq-com-2d"/>, so the centroid depends only on the shape of the
object: to find a centroid we may as well take <m>\delta = 1</m>, and
then the mass <m>M</m> is simply the area of the plate.
</p>

<example xml:id="example-centroid-between-curves">
<title>Centroid of the Region Between Two Curves</title>
<statement>
<p>
Find the centroid of the region between <m>y = \sqrt{x}</m> and
<m>y = x^2</m>.
</p>
</statement>
</example>

<solution xml:id="solution-centroid-between-curves">
<p>
The two curves intersect where <m>\sqrt{x} = x^2</m>, that is, at
<m>x = 0</m> and <m>x = 1</m>, so the region lies over the interval
<m>0 \le x \le 1</m> with <m>x^2 \le y \le \sqrt{x}</m>, as shown in
<xref ref="fig-centroid-between-curves"/>. Since we are finding the
centroid, we may take the constant density to be <m>\delta = 1</m>,
in which case, by <xref ref="eq-first-moments-2d"/>, the mass is simply
the area:
</p>

<me>
M = \int_0^1 \int_{x^2}^{\sqrt{x}} dy\, dx
  = \int_0^1 \left( \sqrt{x} - x^2 \right) dx
  = \left[ \frac{2}{3}x^{3/2} - \frac{x^3}{3} \right]_0^1
  = \frac{1}{3}.
</me>

<p>
By <xref ref="eq-first-moments-2d"/>, the moment about the
<m>y</m>-axis is
</p>

<me>
M_y = \int_0^1 \int_{x^2}^{\sqrt{x}} x\, dy\, dx
    = \int_0^1 x \left( \sqrt{x} - x^2 \right) dx
    = \int_0^1 \left( x^{3/2} - x^3 \right) dx
    = \frac{2}{5} - \frac{1}{4}
    = \frac{3}{20},
</me>

<p>
and the moment about the <m>x</m>-axis is
</p>

<me>
M_x = \int_0^1 \int_{x^2}^{\sqrt{x}} y\, dy\, dx
    = \int_0^1 \frac{(\sqrt{x})^2 - (x^2)^2}{2}\, dx
    = \frac{1}{2} \int_0^1 \left( x - x^4 \right) dx
    = \frac{1}{2}\left( \frac{1}{2} - \frac{1}{5} \right)
    = \frac{3}{20}.
</me>

<p>
By <xref ref="eq-com-2d"/>,
</p>

<me>
\bar{x} = \frac{M_y}{M} = \frac{3/20}{1/3} = \frac{9}{20},
\qquad
\bar{y} = \frac{M_x}{M} = \frac{3/20}{1/3} = \frac{9}{20},
</me>

<p>
so the centroid is <m>\left( \tfrac{9}{20}, \tfrac{9}{20} \right)</m>.
It is no accident that <m>\bar{x} = \bar{y}</m>: the region is symmetric
about the line <m>y = x</m>, so its centroid must lie on that line. See
<xref ref="fig-centroid-between-curves-video"/> for an animated
walkthrough.
</p>
</solution>

<figure xml:id="fig-centroid-between-curves-video">
<caption>
The region between <m>y = \sqrt{x}</m> and <m>y = x^2</m> is symmetric
about the line <m>y = x</m>, and its centroid
<m>\left( \tfrac{9}{20}, \tfrac{9}{20} \right)</m> lies on that line.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-centroid-between-curves"
       source="videos/centroid_between_curves_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-centroid-between-curves">
<caption>
The region between <m>y = \sqrt{x}</m> (blue) and <m>y = x^2</m> (red)
over <m>0 \le x \le 1</m>, with its centroid at
<m>\left( \tfrac{9}{20}, \tfrac{9}{20} \right)</m>.
</caption>
<image width="55%">
<shortdescription>
The region between the square-root curve and the parabola, from 0 to 1.
</shortdescription>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-centroid-between-curves">
<diagram dimensions="(320,320)" margins="10">
<definition>f(x) = sqrt(x)</definition>
<definition>g(x) = x^2</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-0.35,-0.35,1.55,1.55)">
<grid-axes at="cb-grid" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
<area-between-curves at="cb-region" functions="(f,g)" domain="(0,1)"
                     fill="#9ecfff" stroke="none"/>
<graph at="cb-sqrt" function="f" domain="(0,1.5)" stroke="blue"
       thickness="2.5"/>
<graph at="cb-square" function="g" domain="(-0.2,1.22)" stroke="red"
       thickness="2.5"/>
<point at="cb-origin" p="(0,0)" fill="black" size="3"/>
<point at="cb-one" clear-background="yes" background-margin="3" p="(1,1)" fill="black" size="3" alignment="se">
<m>(1,1)</m>
</point>
<point at="cb-centroid" p="(0.45,0.45)" fill="maroon" size="4"/>
<label at="cb-centroid-label" anchor="(0.5,0.4)" alignment="se"
       clear-background="yes" background-margin="4">
<m>\left(\frac{9}{20},\frac{9}{20}\right)</m>
</label>
<label at="cb-sqrt-label" anchor="(1.5,0.72)" alignment="sw"
       color="blue">
<m>y=\sqrt{x}</m>
</label>
<label at="cb-square-label" anchor="(1.1,1.4)" alignment="w"
       color="red">
<m>y=x^2</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A coordinate grid showing the region between the curves y equals the square root of x, in blue, and y equals x squared, in red. The curves intersect at the origin and at the point 1 comma 1, enclosing a lens-shaped region shaded light blue. The centroid of the region, at 9 twentieths comma 9 twentieths, is marked in maroon.">
  <annotation ref="cb-sqrt" text="The blue curve y equals the square root of x, the upper boundary of the region."/>
  <annotation ref="cb-square" text="The red curve y equals x squared, the lower boundary of the region."/>
  <annotation ref="cb-region" text="The lens-shaped region between the two curves, shaded light blue, lying over the interval from 0 to 1."/>
  <annotation ref="cb-one" text="The point 1 comma 1, where the two curves intersect."/>
  <annotation ref="cb-centroid" text="The centroid of the region at 9 twentieths comma 9 twentieths, marked in maroon. It lies on the line y equals x because the region is symmetric about that line."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<example xml:id="example-cardioid-centroid">
<title>Centroid of a Cardioid</title>
<statement>
<p>
Find the centroid of the object that has the shape
<m>r = 1 + \cos(\theta)</m> and density <m>\delta = 2</m>.
</p>
</statement>
</example>

<solution xml:id="solution-cardioid-centroid">
<p>
The cardioid <m>r = 1 + \cos(\theta)</m> is shown in
<xref ref="fig-cardioid-centroid"/>. The density is constant, so we are
indeed finding a centroid, and the answer will not depend on the value
<m>\delta = 2</m>. The region is symmetric about the <m>x</m>-axis
(replacing <m>\theta</m> by <m>-\theta</m> does not change <m>r</m>),
so <m>\bar{y} = 0</m> and we only need <m>\bar{x}</m>.
</p>

<p>
Working in polar coordinates with <m>dA = r\, dr\, d\theta</m> from
<xref ref="eq-polar-area-element"/>, the mass
<xref ref="eq-first-moments-2d"/> is
</p>

<md>
<mrow>M \amp= \iint_R \delta\, dA
= 2 \int_0^{2\pi} \int_0^{1+\cos\theta} r\, dr\, d\theta
= \int_0^{2\pi} (1 + \cos\theta)^2\, d\theta</mrow>
<mrow>\amp= \int_0^{2\pi} \left( 1 + 2\cos\theta + \cos^2\theta \right) d\theta
= 2\pi + 0 + \pi = 3\pi.</mrow>
</md>

<p>
Since <m>x = r\cos\theta</m> by <xref ref="eq-polar-cartesian"/>, the
moment about the <m>y</m>-axis is
</p>

<md>
<mrow>M_y \amp= \iint_R x\, \delta\, dA
= 2 \int_0^{2\pi} \int_0^{1+\cos\theta} (r\cos\theta)\, r\, dr\, d\theta
= \frac{2}{3} \int_0^{2\pi} \cos\theta\, (1 + \cos\theta)^3\, d\theta</mrow>
<mrow>\amp= \frac{2}{3} \int_0^{2\pi} \left( \cos\theta + 3\cos^2\theta
+ 3\cos^3\theta + \cos^4\theta \right) d\theta
= \frac{2}{3} \left( 0 + 3\pi + 0 + \frac{3\pi}{4} \right)
= \frac{5\pi}{2},</mrow>
</md>

<p>
where the odd powers of cosine integrate to zero over a full period,
<m>\int_0^{2\pi} \cos^2\theta\, d\theta = \pi</m>, and
<m>\int_0^{2\pi} \cos^4\theta\, d\theta = \tfrac{3\pi}{4}</m>. By
<xref ref="eq-com-2d"/>,
</p>

<me>
\bar{x} = \frac{M_y}{M} = \frac{5\pi/2}{3\pi} = \frac{5}{6},
</me>

<p>
so the centroid is <m>\left( \tfrac{5}{6}, 0 \right)</m>. As promised,
the constant density <m>\delta = 2</m> canceled out. The animation in
<xref ref="fig-cardioid-centroid-video"/> shows how symmetry and the
polar integrals combine to locate this point.
</p>
</solution>

<figure xml:id="fig-cardioid-centroid-video">
<caption>
The cardioid <m>r = 1 + \cos(\theta)</m> with constant density: symmetry
places the centroid on the <m>x</m>-axis, and the polar moment integrals
put it at <m>\left( \tfrac{5}{6}, 0 \right)</m>.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-cardioid-centroid"
       source="videos/cardioid_centroid_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-cardioid-centroid">
<caption>
The cardioid <m>r = 1 + \cos(\theta)</m>. The region is symmetric about
the <m>x</m>-axis and its centroid <m>\left( \tfrac{5}{6}, 0 \right)</m>
is marked in maroon.
</caption>
<image width="55%">
<shortdescription>
A cardioid symmetric about the x axis with shaded interior.
</shortdescription>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-cardioid-centroid">
<diagram dimensions="(360,300)" margins="10">
<definition>c(t) = ((1 + cos(t))*cos(t), (1 + cos(t))*sin(t))</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-0.9,-1.55,2.65,1.55)">
<axes at="cd-axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" arrows="1"/>
<parametric-curve at="cd-cardioid" function="c" domain="(0,2*pi)"
                  closed="yes" fill="#9ecfff" stroke="blue"
                  thickness="2.5"/>
<point at="cd-centroid" clear-background="yes" background-margin="3" p="(0.8333,0)" fill="maroon" size="4"
       alignment="ne">
<m>\left(\frac{5}{6},0\right)</m>
</point>
<label at="cd-curve-label" clear-background="yes" background-margin="4" anchor="(1.55,-1.05)" alignment="se"
       color="blue">
<m>r=1+\cos(\theta)</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A cardioid, the heart-shaped curve r equals 1 plus cosine theta, drawn in blue with its interior shaded light blue. It is symmetric about the x axis, with a cusp at the origin and its widest point at x equals 2. The centroid, at five sixths comma 0, is marked in maroon on the x axis.">
  <annotation ref="cd-cardioid" text="The cardioid r equals 1 plus cosine theta, a closed heart-shaped curve with a cusp at the origin, shaded light blue."/>
  <annotation ref="cd-centroid" text="The centroid of the cardioid at five sixths comma 0, marked in maroon. It lies on the x axis because the region is symmetric about that axis."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-mass-3d-object">
<title>Finding the Center of Mass of a 3D Object*</title>

<p>
Everything so far took place in the plane. For point masses in space,
with <m>\mathbf{r}_i = (x_i, y_i, z_i)</m> in
<xref ref="eq-com-points-vector"/>, the distances are measured from the
coordinate planes instead of the coordinate axes. The
<term>first moments about the coordinate planes</term> are
</p>

<men xml:id="eq-first-moments-points-3d">
M_{yz} = \sum_{i=1}^{n} m_i\, x_i, \qquad
M_{xz} = \sum_{i=1}^{n} m_i\, y_i, \qquad
M_{xy} = \sum_{i=1}^{n} m_i\, z_i
</men>

<p>
(<m>M_{yz}</m> uses the signed distance <m>x_i</m> from the
<m>yz</m>-plane, and so on), and the center of mass
<m>\bar{\mathbf{r}} = (\bar{x}, \bar{y}, \bar{z})</m> is
</p>

<men xml:id="eq-com-points-3d">
\bar{x} = \frac{M_{yz}}{M}, \qquad
\bar{y} = \frac{M_{xz}}{M}, \qquad
\bar{z} = \frac{M_{xy}}{M}.
</men>

<p>
Now let a solid object occupy a region <m>D</m> in space, with
density <m>\delta(x,y,z)</m> (mass per unit volume) at the point
<m>(x,y,z)</m>. The plan is the same as for a plate, one dimension up:
we cut the solid into small boxes and treat each box as a point mass.
Partition space into boxes by the planes <m>x = x_0, \dots, x_n</m>,
<m>y = y_0, \dots, y_m</m>, and <m>z = z_0, \dots, z_p</m>. The box in
position <m>i</m> along the <m>x</m>-direction, <m>j</m> along the
<m>y</m>-direction, and <m>k</m> along the <m>z</m>-direction has the
dimensions <m>\Delta x_i</m>, <m>\Delta y_j</m>, <m>\Delta z_k</m> and the
volume <m>\Delta V_{ijk} = \Delta x_i\, \Delta y_j\, \Delta z_k</m>; if
it meets <m>D</m>, we pick a point <m>(x_i, y_j, z_k)</m> in it, as in
<xref ref="fig-mass-element-tikz"/>. The density is nearly constant on so
small a box, so its mass is approximately
</p>

<me>
\Delta m_{ijk} = \delta(x_i, y_j, z_k)\, \Delta V_{ijk}
= \delta(x_i, y_j, z_k)\, \Delta x_i\, \Delta y_j\, \Delta z_k .
</me>

<p>
The solid is approximated by the system of point masses
<m>\Delta m_{ijk}</m> at the points <m>(x_i, y_j, z_k)</m>, one for each
box that meets <m>D</m>, so <xref ref="eq-com-points-vector"/> and
<xref ref="eq-first-moments-points-3d"/> give its mass and its first
moments as triple sums; for instance,
</p>

<md>
<mrow>M \amp\approx \sum_{k=1}^{p}\sum_{j=1}^{m}\sum_{i=1}^{n}
  \delta(x_i, y_j, z_k)\, \Delta x_i\, \Delta y_j\, \Delta z_k ,</mrow>
<mrow>M_{xy} \amp\approx \sum_{k=1}^{p}\sum_{j=1}^{m}\sum_{i=1}^{n}
  z_k\, \delta(x_i, y_j, z_k)\, \Delta x_i\, \Delta y_j\, \Delta z_k ,</mrow>
</md>

<p>
the sums running over the boxes that meet <m>D</m>. (Newton's second law
for this system of boxes reads
<m>\mathbf{F} = M\, \dfrac{d^2\bar{\mathbf{r}}}{dt^2}</m>, as in the
previous subsections: the solid moves as if its whole mass were
concentrated at its center of mass.) These are Riemann sums, and letting
the boxes shrink, <m>n, m, p \to \infty</m>, turns each triple sum into a
triple integral over <m>D</m>. For the mass,
</p>

<men xml:id="eq-mass-3d">
M = \lim_{n,m,p \to \infty} \sum_{k=1}^{p}\sum_{j=1}^{m}\sum_{i=1}^{n}
  \delta(x_i, y_j, z_k)\, \Delta x_i\, \Delta y_j\, \Delta z_k
  = \iiint_D \delta(x,y,z)\, dV.
</men>

<p>
Watch the construction unfold in <xref ref="fig-mass-element-video"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-mass-element-video">
<caption>
Partitioning a solid <m>D</m> into small boxes: each box carries the
mass <q>density times volume</q>, and the Riemann sum of these masses
converges to the triple integral of the density.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-mass-element"
       source="videos/mass_element_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-mass-element-tikz">
<caption>
A solid region <m>D</m> with a small box around the point
<m>(x_i, y_j, z_k)</m>. The box has the volume <m>\Delta V_{ijk}</m> and
the mass <m>\Delta m_{ijk} = \delta(x_i, y_j, z_k)\, \Delta V_{ijk}</m>.
</caption>
<image xml:id="image-mass-element-tikz" width="75%">
<shortdescription>
A potato-shaped solid in space with a small highlighted box inside it.
</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
A translucent potato-shaped solid labeled <m>D</m> floats above the
<m>xy</m>-plane in a three-dimensional coordinate system. A small
purple box inside the solid marks the point <m>(x_i, y_j, z_k)</m>,
and a leader line labels its mass
<m>\Delta m_{ijk} = \delta(x_i, y_j, z_k)\, \Delta V_{ijk}</m>.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image>
<![CDATA[\begin{tikzpicture}[line join=round, line cap=round]
  \tikzset{lbl/.style={fill=white, fill opacity=0.82, text opacity=1, inner sep=2.5pt, rounded corners=2pt}}
  % Okabe-Ito colorblind-safe palette
  \definecolor{oigreen}{RGB}{0,158,115}
  \definecolor{oipurple}{RGB}{204,121,167}
  \begin{axis}[
      view={115}{18},
      width=12cm,
      axis lines=center,
      axis equal image,
      axis line style={-latex, black!75, line width=0.7pt},
      xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, zlabel={$z$},
      label style={font=\large},
      ticks=none,
      xmin=-2.9, xmax=2.9, ymin=-2.9, ymax=2.9, zmin=0, zmax=4.1,
      clip=false,
    ]
    % ---- the solid D: a deformed (potato-like) ellipsoid ----
    \addplot3[surf, shader=interp,
              point meta={z},
              colormap={softg}{color=(oigreen!14!white) color=(oigreen!48!white)},
              opacity=0.55, fill opacity=0.55,
              domain=0:360, samples=49,
              y domain=0:180, samples y=25,
              z buffer=sort, forget plot]
      ({(2+0.35*sin(2*x))*cos(x)*sin(y)},
       {(1.35+0.2*cos(2*x))*sin(x)*sin(y)},
       {0.95*cos(y)+2.3});
    % ---- small box (mass element) inside the solid ----
    % cube centered near (1.05, 0.55, 2.6) with half-side 0.21
    \def\cx{1.05}\def\cy{0.55}\def\cz{2.60}\def\hs{0.21}
    % top face
    \fill[oipurple!70, draw=oipurple!35!black, line width=0.5pt]
      (axis cs:{\cx-\hs},{\cy-\hs},{\cz+\hs}) --
      (axis cs:{\cx+\hs},{\cy-\hs},{\cz+\hs}) --
      (axis cs:{\cx+\hs},{\cy+\hs},{\cz+\hs}) --
      (axis cs:{\cx-\hs},{\cy+\hs},{\cz+\hs}) -- cycle;
    % front face (toward viewer, +x side)
    \fill[oipurple!45, draw=oipurple!35!black, line width=0.5pt]
      (axis cs:{\cx+\hs},{\cy-\hs},{\cz-\hs}) --
      (axis cs:{\cx+\hs},{\cy+\hs},{\cz-\hs}) --
      (axis cs:{\cx+\hs},{\cy+\hs},{\cz+\hs}) --
      (axis cs:{\cx+\hs},{\cy-\hs},{\cz+\hs}) -- cycle;
    % side face (+y side)
    \fill[oipurple!30, draw=oipurple!35!black, line width=0.5pt]
      (axis cs:{\cx-\hs},{\cy+\hs},{\cz-\hs}) --
      (axis cs:{\cx+\hs},{\cy+\hs},{\cz-\hs}) --
      (axis cs:{\cx+\hs},{\cy+\hs},{\cz+\hs}) --
      (axis cs:{\cx-\hs},{\cy+\hs},{\cz+\hs}) -- cycle;
    % ---- labels ----
    \node[lbl, font=\large] at (axis cs:-1.7,-0.4,3.1) {$D$};
    \draw[black!70, line width=0.5pt]
      (axis cs:{\cx},{\cy+\hs},{\cz+\hs}) -- (axis cs:1.6,2.0,3.7);
    \node[lbl, font=\normalsize, anchor=west] at (axis cs:1.6,2.0,3.7)
      {$\Delta m_{ijk} = \delta(x_i,y_j,z_k)\,\Delta V_{ijk}$};
    \draw[black!70, line width=0.5pt]
      (axis cs:{\cx},{\cy+\hs},{\cz-\hs}) -- (axis cs:1.2,2.3,1.9);
    \node[lbl, font=\normalsize, anchor=west] at (axis cs:1.2,2.3,1.9)
      {$(x_i, y_j, z_k)$};
  \end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}]]>
</latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
In the same way, the first moments become the triple integrals
</p>

<men xml:id="eq-first-moments-3d">
M_{yz} = \iiint_D x\, \delta\, dV, \qquad
M_{xz} = \iiint_D y\, \delta\, dV, \qquad
M_{xy} = \iiint_D z\, \delta\, dV,
</men>

<p>
the moments about the <m>yz</m>-, <m>xz</m>-, and <m>xy</m>-planes, and
the center of mass <m>(\bar{x}, \bar{y}, \bar{z})</m> of the solid is
the point whose coordinates are the moments divided by the total mass:
</p>

<men xml:id="eq-com-3d">
\bar{x} = \frac{M_{yz}}{M}, \qquad
\bar{y} = \frac{M_{xz}}{M}, \qquad
\bar{z} = \frac{M_{xy}}{M}.
</men>

<example xml:id="example-paraboloid-com">
<title>Center of Mass of a Solid Bounded by a Paraboloid</title>
<statement>
<p>
Find the center of mass of a solid of constant density <m>\delta</m>,
which is bounded by the disk <m>x^2 + y^2 \le 4</m> (in the plane
<m>z = 0</m>) and the paraboloid <m>z = 4 - x^2 - y^2</m>.
</p>
</statement>
</example>

<solution xml:id="solution-paraboloid-com">
<p>
The solid is shown in <xref ref="fig-paraboloid-com-tikz"/>. Both the
region and the density are symmetric about the <m>z</m>-axis, so the
center of mass lies on that axis: <m>\bar{x} = \bar{y} = 0</m>. It
remains to find <m>\bar{z} = M_{xy}/M</m> from
<xref ref="eq-com-3d"/>.
</p>

<p>
Because of the circular symmetry, we work in cylindrical coordinates,
where <xref ref="eq-dv-cylindrical"/> applies and the region is
described by
<m>0 \le z \le 4 - r^2</m>, <m>0 \le r \le 2</m>, and
<m>0 \le \theta \le 2\pi</m>. By <xref ref="eq-mass-3d"/>, the mass is
</p>

<md>
<mrow>M \amp= \iiint_D \delta\, dV
= \delta \int_0^{2\pi} \int_0^2 \int_0^{4-r^2} dz\, r\, dr\, d\theta</mrow>
<mrow>\amp= \delta \int_0^{2\pi} \int_0^2 (4 - r^2)\, r\, dr\, d\theta
= \delta \int_0^{2\pi} \left[ 2r^2 - \frac{r^4}{4} \right]_0^2 d\theta</mrow>
<mrow>\amp= \delta \int_0^{2\pi} 4\, d\theta = 8\pi\delta.</mrow>
</md>

<p>
Using <xref ref="eq-first-moments-3d"/>, the first moment about the
<m>xy</m>-plane is
</p>

<md>
<mrow>M_{xy} \amp= \iiint_D z\, \delta\, dV
= \delta \int_0^{2\pi} \int_0^2 \int_0^{4-r^2} z\, dz\, r\, dr\, d\theta</mrow>
<mrow>\amp= \delta \int_0^{2\pi} \int_0^2 \frac{(4 - r^2)^2}{2}\, r\, dr\, d\theta
= \delta \int_0^{2\pi} \left[ -\frac{(4-r^2)^3}{12} \right]_0^2 d\theta</mrow>
<mrow>\amp= \delta \int_0^{2\pi} \frac{16}{3}\, d\theta = \frac{32\pi\delta}{3}.</mrow>
</md>

<p>
Therefore
</p>

<me>
\bar{z} = \frac{M_{xy}}{M} = \frac{32\pi\delta/3}{8\pi\delta} = \frac{4}{3},
</me>

<p>
and the center of mass is <m>\left(0, 0, \tfrac{4}{3}\right)</m>: since
the density is constant, this point is the centroid of the solid. Notice
that the answer does not depend on <m>\delta</m>, as a centroid never
does: for an object of constant density, the location of the balance
point is determined by the shape alone. Watch the computation come
together in <xref ref="fig-paraboloid-com-video"/>.
</p>
</solution>

<figure xml:id="fig-paraboloid-com-video">
<caption>
The solid bounded above by <m>z = 4 - x^2 - y^2</m> and below by the
disk <m>x^2 + y^2 \le 4</m>. By symmetry the center of mass lies on the
<m>z</m>-axis, at height <m>\bar{z} = 4/3</m>.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-paraboloid-com"
       source="videos/paraboloid_com_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-paraboloid-com-tikz">
<caption>
The solid of constant density bounded by the paraboloid
<m>z = 4 - x^2 - y^2</m> and the disk <m>R</m>: <m>x^2 + y^2 \le 4</m>
in the plane <m>z = 0</m>. The center of mass sits on the axis of
symmetry at <m>(0, 0, 4/3)</m>.
</caption>
<image xml:id="image-paraboloid-com-tikz" width="80%">
<shortdescription>
A dome-shaped solid over a disk with its center of mass marked on the
vertical axis.
</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
The paraboloid <m>z = 4 - x^2 - y^2</m> forms a translucent green dome
over the disk <m>x^2 + y^2 \le 4</m> in the <m>xy</m>-plane, labeled
<m>R</m>. The dashed <m>z</m>-axis passes through the apex at height 4,
and a magenta dot on the axis at height <m>4/3</m> marks the center of
mass.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image>
<![CDATA[\begin{tikzpicture}[line join=round, line cap=round]
  \tikzset{lbl/.style={fill=white, fill opacity=0.82, text opacity=1, inner sep=2.5pt, rounded corners=2pt}}
  % Okabe-Ito colorblind-safe palette
  \definecolor{oigreen}{RGB}{0,158,115}
  \definecolor{oiblue}{RGB}{0,114,178}
  \definecolor{oimagenta}{RGB}{204,0,119}
  \begin{axis}[
      view={115}{18},
      width=12.5cm,
      axis lines=center,
      axis equal image,
      axis line style={-latex, black!75, line width=0.7pt},
      xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, zlabel={$z$},
      label style={font=\large},
      ticks=none,
      xmin=-3, xmax=3, ymin=-3, ymax=3, zmin=0, zmax=4.6,
      clip=false,
    ]
    % ---- base disk R ----
    \addplot3[surf, shader=flat,
              color=black!12, opacity=0.4, fill opacity=0.4,
              domain=0:360, samples=37,
              y domain=0:2, samples y=2,
              z buffer=sort, forget plot]
      ({y*cos(x)}, {y*sin(x)}, {0});
    % ---- paraboloid surface ----
    \addplot3[surf, shader=interp,
              point meta={z},
              colormap={softg}{color=(oigreen!14!white) color=(oigreen!48!white)},
              opacity=0.55, fill opacity=0.55,
              domain=0:360, samples=61,
              y domain=0:2, samples y=25,
              z buffer=sort, forget plot]
      ({y*cos(x)}, {y*sin(x)}, {4 - y^2});
    % light parallels for shape cues
    \foreach \rr in {0.5,1,1.5}{
      \edef\tmp{\noexpand\addplot3[black!22, line width=0.35pt, domain=0:360,
          samples=73, samples y=0, forget plot]
        ({\rr*cos(x)}, {\rr*sin(x)}, {4 - \rr*\rr});}\tmp}
    % light meridians for shape cues
    \foreach \uu in {0,45,...,315}{
      \edef\tmp{\noexpand\addplot3[black!22, line width=0.35pt, domain=0:2,
          samples=25, samples y=0, forget plot]
        ({x*cos(\uu)}, {x*sin(\uu)}, {4 - x^2});}\tmp}
    % ---- boundary circle x^2 + y^2 = 4 ----
    \addplot3[line width=1.6pt, oiblue, domain=0:360, samples=121,
        samples y=0, forget plot]
      ({2*cos(x)}, {2*sin(x)}, {0});
    % ---- dashed axis of symmetry through the solid ----
    \draw[dashed, black!60, line width=0.7pt]
      (axis cs:0,0,0) -- (axis cs:0,0,4);
    % ---- center of mass ----
    \fill[oimagenta] (axis cs:0,0,1.3333) circle (2.6pt);
    \node[lbl, font=\normalsize, anchor=west, text=oimagenta]
      at (axis cs:0.15,0.55,1.55) {c.m. $\left(0,0,\tfrac{4}{3}\right)$};
    % ---- labels ----
    \draw[black!70, line width=0.5pt]
      (axis cs:{1.35*cos(35)},{1.35*sin(35)},{4-1.35^2}) -- (axis cs:1.9,2.5,3.4);
    \node[lbl, font=\normalsize, anchor=west] at (axis cs:1.9,2.5,3.4)
      {$z = 4 - x^2 - y^2$};
    \draw[black!70, line width=0.5pt]
      (axis cs:{2*cos(320)},{2*sin(320)},0) -- (axis cs:1.4,-2.75,0.75);
    \node[lbl, font=\normalsize, anchor=east] at (axis cs:1.4,-2.75,0.75)
      {$x^2 + y^2 = 4$};
    \node[lbl, font=\large] at (axis cs:1.15,1.5,0) {$R$};
    \node[lbl, font=\normalsize] at (axis cs:-0.3,-0.25,0) {$0$};
  \end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}]]>
</latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="subsec-mass-questions">
<title>Concept Check</title>

<p>
The first problem is about point masses, where everything can be done by
hand in a line or two; the second revisits the region of
<xref ref="example-centroid-between-curves"/> with two densities that are
not constant.
</p>

<exercise xml:id="q-mass-three-points">
<title>Three point masses</title>
<statement>
<p>
Masses of <m>1</m>, <m>2</m>, and <m>3</m> kilograms sit at the points
<m>(0,0)</m>, <m>(4,0)</m>, and <m>(0,4)</m>, respectively. Find the
center of mass of the system. Where would it be if the three masses were
equal?
</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>
By <xref ref="eq-first-moments-points"/> and
<xref ref="eq-com-points"/>, with <m>M = 1 + 2 + 3 = 6</m>,
</p>
<me>
M_y = 1\cdot 0 + 2\cdot 4 + 3\cdot 0 = 8, \qquad
M_x = 1\cdot 0 + 2\cdot 0 + 3\cdot 4 = 12,
\qquad\text{so}\qquad
\bar{x} = \frac{8}{6} = \frac{4}{3}, \quad \bar{y} = \frac{12}{6} = 2 .
</me>
<p>
The center of mass <m>\left(\tfrac{4}{3}, 2\right)</m> lies inside the
triangle with the masses at its vertices, pulled toward the heaviest
mass at <m>(0,4)</m>: <m>\bar{y} = 2</m> exceeds <m>\bar{x} = 4/3</m>
because the mass at <m>(0,4)</m> is heavier than the one at
<m>(4,0)</m>. With three equal masses <m>m</m>, every weight
<m>m_i/M</m> in <xref ref="eq-com-points-vector"/> is <m>1/3</m>, and the
center of mass is the average of the three positions,
<m>\left( \tfrac{0+4+0}{3}, \tfrac{0+0+4}{3} \right)
= \left( \tfrac{4}{3}, \tfrac{4}{3} \right)</m>: the centroid of the
triangle, where its three medians meet.
</p>
</answer>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="q-mass-two-densities">
<title>The same plate, two densities</title>
<statement>
<p>
Find the center of mass of the region between <m>y = \sqrt{x}</m> and
<m>y = x^2</m>, assuming that the density function is
</p>
<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li><p><m>\delta_1(x,y) = xy</m>;</p></li>
<li><p><m>\delta_2(x,y) = y</m>.</p></li>
</ol>
</p>
<p>
<em>Question:</em> Do your answers in parts A and B match your intuition?
If so, justify your answer in each case. (For comparison, the centroid of
this region is <m>\left(\tfrac{9}{20}, \tfrac{9}{20}\right)</m>, by
<xref ref="example-centroid-between-curves"/>.)
</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>
The region is <m>0 \le x \le 1</m>, <m>x^2 \le y \le \sqrt{x}</m>, and
we use <xref ref="eq-first-moments-2d"/> and <xref ref="eq-com-2d"/>.
</p>
<p>
<em>A.</em> With <m>\delta_1 = xy</m>,
</p>
<md>
<mrow>M \amp= \int_0^1 \int_{x^2}^{\sqrt{x}} xy\, dy\, dx
= \int_0^1 \frac{x\,(x - x^4)}{2}\, dx
= \frac{1}{2}\left( \frac{1}{3} - \frac{1}{6} \right) = \frac{1}{12},</mrow>
<mrow>M_y \amp= \int_0^1 \int_{x^2}^{\sqrt{x}} x^2 y\, dy\, dx
= \int_0^1 \frac{x^2\,(x - x^4)}{2}\, dx
= \frac{1}{2}\left( \frac{1}{4} - \frac{1}{7} \right) = \frac{3}{56},</mrow>
<mrow>M_x \amp= \int_0^1 \int_{x^2}^{\sqrt{x}} x y^2\, dy\, dx
= \int_0^1 \frac{x\,(x^{3/2} - x^6)}{3}\, dx
= \frac{1}{3}\left( \frac{2}{7} - \frac{1}{8} \right) = \frac{3}{56},</mrow>
</md>
<p>
so <m>\bar{x} = \bar{y} = \dfrac{3/56}{1/12} = \dfrac{9}{14} \approx 0.64</m>.
This fits: the density <m>xy</m> is largest near the corner <m>(1,1)</m>
of the region and vanishes at the origin, so the balance point moves
from the centroid <m>(9/20, 9/20)</m> toward <m>(1,1)</m>; and it stays
on the line <m>y = x</m>, because swapping <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> changes
neither the region nor the density <m>xy</m>.
</p>
<p>
<em>B.</em> With <m>\delta_2 = y</m>,
</p>
<md>
<mrow>M \amp= \int_0^1 \int_{x^2}^{\sqrt{x}} y\, dy\, dx
= \int_0^1 \frac{x - x^4}{2}\, dx
= \frac{1}{2}\left( \frac{1}{2} - \frac{1}{5} \right) = \frac{3}{20},</mrow>
<mrow>M_y \amp= \int_0^1 \int_{x^2}^{\sqrt{x}} xy\, dy\, dx
= \frac{1}{12} \quad\text{(the mass of part A)},</mrow>
<mrow>M_x \amp= \int_0^1 \int_{x^2}^{\sqrt{x}} y^2\, dy\, dx
= \int_0^1 \frac{x^{3/2} - x^6}{3}\, dx
= \frac{1}{3}\left( \frac{2}{5} - \frac{1}{7} \right) = \frac{3}{35},</mrow>
</md>
<p>
so <m>\bar{x} = \dfrac{1/12}{3/20} = \dfrac{5}{9} \approx 0.56</m> and
<m>\bar{y} = \dfrac{3/35}{3/20} = \dfrac{4}{7} \approx 0.57</m>. This fits
too: the density grows with <m>y</m>, so the mass sits higher than in a
uniform plate, and <m>\bar{y}</m> rises from <m>9/20</m> to <m>4/7</m>.
The center of mass now lies slightly above the line <m>y = x</m>: the
density <m>y</m> treats <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> differently, so nothing
forces the balance point onto that line. (<m>\bar{x}</m> rises as well,
from <m>9/20</m> to <m>5/9</m>, because the heavier upper part of this
region is also its right part.)
</p>
</answer>
</exercise>

</subsection>

</section>
